<div style="width: 100%; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; text-align: center; padding: 15px 0; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 24px;">Lesson Planner – Theme: These Plants Are from Panama. – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 16px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping in the Garden</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> These Plants Are from Panama.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Write simple role plays or dialogues collaboratively.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can express opinions in writing about garden tasks in short sentences (e.g., 'I like watering the plants, but I don't like digging.').<br>Can write simple role plays or dialogues about garden tasks with classmates (e.g., 'How do you plant the tomatoes?' 'You dig a hole, plant the seed, and water the plant.').<br>Can collaborate to revise dialogues for clarity (e.g., Add 'water the plants' to the dialogue.).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of garden items (e.g., 'tomato,' 'seed,' 'soil,' 'watering can'). Students quickly write the name of each item on mini whiteboards or a piece of paper. The teacher calls out words and asks students to spell them aloud, focusing on phonemic awareness for words like 'water,' 'sun,' 'soil'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher displays a simple written dialogue on the board or projector, e.g., "A: Do you like watering plants? B: Yes, I like watering plants, but I don't like digging." The teacher models writing the sentence structure for expressing preferences and simple questions, highlighting 'like' and 'don't like', and how to form a question about garden tasks using 'Do you...'. The teacher also writes a short instruction, e.g., "First, dig a hole." and points out the sequence word.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b><br>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I write 'I like watering the plants,' does it mean I enjoy it?" (Expected answer: Yes)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I write 'First, plant the seed,' what do we do before planting?" (Expected answer: Nothing / It's the first step)</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated written dialogue about two friends helping in a Panamanian garden (e.g., 'These are tomatoes, and this is a tomato plant.') and describing planting steps. The dialogue includes present simple + 's' and question forms.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing simple gardening instructions: Using conjunctions to describe gardening tasks (e.g., 'You dig a hole, plant the seed, and water the plant.').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the dialogue silently.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are the children doing in the garden?" (Students write their answer on a sticky note).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Matching:</b> Students match sentences from the dialogue to pictures of the gardening steps (e.g., 'dig a hole' to a picture of digging, 'plant the seed' to a picture of planting).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students write 'T' or 'F' next to statements about the dialogue (e.g., 'They plant flowers.' 'They water the plants every day.').</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing simple sentences using present simple, demonstratives, question forms, sequence words, and expressing preferences with 'like'/'don't like', using vocabulary related to gardening.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Scramble and Gap-fill:</b> Students receive strips of paper with jumbled words to form simple sentences about garden tasks (e.g., "the / water / plants / I / every day" -> "I water the plants every day."). Then, they complete sentences with missing garden verbs or nouns (e.g., "First, we ____ the seeds in the ____." - plant, soil).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Writing Preferences:</b> Students complete sentences expressing preferences based on picture prompts. (e.g., Picture of watering: "I like ____ing the plants." Picture of digging: "I don't like ____ing.") They then rewrite two sentences, changing the subject to "He" or "She" and adjusting the verb (e.g., "He likes watering the plants.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Collaborative Sentence Building:</b> In pairs, students receive a sentence starter (e.g., "These are...") and must collaboratively write a complete sentence using a demonstrative and a gardening noun (e.g., "These are green plants."). They then write a question for their partner (e.g., "How often do you water the flowers?").</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Dialogue Writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students work in pairs to write a short dialogue (4-6 exchanges) between two friends helping in a garden. The dialogue must include:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least one question about a garden task (e.g., "How do you plant the tomatoes?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A description of gardening steps using sequence words (e.g., "First, you dig a hole, then you put the seed.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">An expression of preference (e.g., "I like watering, but I don't like digging.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of demonstratives (e.g., "These are healthy plants.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least two different vocabulary words from the list (e.g., 'plant,' 'soil,' 'seed,' 'grow').</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, offering support with spelling and grammar, encouraging collaboration, and reminding students to use the target language. Students practice reading their dialogues quietly to check for clarity and flow.</p></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">12-15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist focusing on written output and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their written dialogues with another pair. They use a simple checklist to peer assess each other's dialogue for clarity and inclusion of specific elements (e.g., "Did they ask a question?", "Did they use 'like' or 'don't like'?"). After peer feedback, pairs revise their dialogues for clarity and accuracy (e.g., "Add 'water the plants' to the dialogue."). The final revised dialogues are submitted.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses target vocabulary words related to gardening.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes simple sentences expressing preferences (like/don't like).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Incorporates question forms (e.g., "How often do you...?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses sequence words (first, second) and discourse markers (and, but).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Dialogue is clear and easy to understand (clarity).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration during writing and revision process.</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a small slip of paper:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word I used in my dialogue?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to write a dialogue about gardening? Why?"</li>
                </ul></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing the dialogue did I do well?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What can I do better next time when writing with a partner?"</li>
                </ul></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The collaborative writing task allowed students to apply grammar and vocabulary effectively. Most students demonstrated understanding of question forms and expressing preferences. Some pairs struggled with incorporating sequence words smoothly. Next time, I will provide more sentence starters or a visual guide for sequencing sentences, or specific sentence frames to guide their writing even more for lower-level learners. The peer assessment was helpful for revision.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will use their written dialogues to create a short role-play or presentation, integrating their written and oral skills to summarize information about Panamanian plants and gardening, applying the 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>